  <blockquote class="evidence">Images must be 500 to 10,000 pixels on the longest side, and "Images with 1,000+ pixels on the longest side enable the zoom function." Read from Amazon's own help article.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central, Product image guide, US store, page undated, read October 17, 2026. https://sellercentral.amazon.com/help/hub/reference/external/G1881</p>

  <p>The file name has three parts separated by periods. Spaces, dashes or extra characters stop the upload. The ID can be the ASIN, UPC, ISBN, EAN, GTIN or JAN. One trap: a file named with an ID and no variant code, B000123456.jpg, uploads as the main image. The guide marks the naming rule optional, and the Upload images page offers flexible naming as well: any file name, assigned to products after upload.</p>

  <p>Amazon contradicts itself in two places, and both matter at upload. Its <a href="https://sellercentral.amazon.com/help/hub/reference/external/G202131130">Product image error codes</a> page says a file must be "at least 500 pixels on the shortest side" and lists .tif, .tiff, .jpg, .jpeg and .gif, with no PNG. The Product image guide, the Upload images page and the upload errors page all say longest side and all accept PNG. That's three pages against one. A file at least 500 pixels on both sides, saved as JPEG, clears every version.</p>

  <p>The fill figure has a history too. Amazon's 2018 Quick Start Style Guide, written by its Australian entity and still linked from the US style guides page, asks for the product to "occupy at least 80 percent of the image area." Build to 85. It passes both.</p>

  <p>Size guides often print a 2,000-pixel recommendation, a 1,600-pixel zoom line or a 10 MB file cap. None of those numbers appears on the Amazon image help pages read for this guide. Amazon's line is 1,000 pixels for zoom, and it sets no megabyte limit there.</p>

  <h2>What can Amazon's additional images show?</h2>

  <p>More than the main image, with a few limits that follow every slot. Amazon names each slot by a variant code, and the code says what belongs in it.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Variant code</th>
          <th scope="col">What Amazon says it is for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">PT01 to PT99</th>
          <td>Additional angles, product in use, screenshots, accessories, product details</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">TOPP, BOTT, LEFT, RGHT, FRNT, BACK, SIDE</th>
          <td>Other angles and sides of the product</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">SWCH</th>
          <td>The swatch, usually a color sample; MAIN stands in when there is none</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">PS01 to PS06</th>
          <td>Warning and safety information, shown under Safety and Product Resources</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">INGR, EEGL, DTLS</th>
          <td>Ingredients, the energy guide, multiples packed together</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">IN01 to IN99</th>
          <td>Interior shots, usually sample pages of books</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Lifestyle scenes belong in the PT slots, as "product in use." What every image still can't carry, main or not: anything that misrepresents the product or doesn't match its title; nudity or sexually suggestive images; customer reviews, five-star graphics, claims such as free shipping, or seller information; Amazon's logos, trademarks or badges, "Best seller" and "Amazon's Choice" included.</p>

  <p>Text is narrower than its reputation. The guide's line reads "No text or pricing details (swatch image only)," so the text ban sits on the swatch, and claims are banned everywhere. Selling in several countries? Text on a country-specific image must be in that country's official language or in English.</p>

  <p>On AI, Amazon's rule is about people, not products: an image showing photorealistic people generated entirely by AI must carry the keyword contains-synthetic-performer in its XMP dc:subject field before upload.</p>

